{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/132425-myths-and-legends-of-yellowstone-park-and-the-surrounding-region","id":132425,"title":"Myths and Legends of Yellowstone Park and the Surrounding Region","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-05-11T10:56:43-07:00","id":132425,"title":"Myths and Legends of Yellowstone Park and the Surrounding Region","content":"So, I may have bitten off more than I can chew with my latest story idea. I'm running the Ragnarok story line and my players just reached Legend 4 and are travelling through the Yellowstone Park area. The party has had some wins and loses, but didn't exactly take their failure to prevent Fimbulvetr very well. So I plan on a rather epic romp through Yellowstone park in which the groups prevents a group of Fire Giants from setting off the Super Volcano under Yellowstone in an attempt to upstage the Frost Giants and their Jotunhammer. I plan to throw in all sorts of crazy things to make it a story to remember, but at this point I'm keeping rather nebulous because I want to incorporate more local legend into it and make the events and places featured more fitting to Yellowstone. Yellowstone itself is a vibrant a living setting that could feature countless stories. There's a lot to do, and I've only got a week to prepare.<br /> <br /> As such I am here to enlist the aid of the community. Help me find good legends, stories, features, and general idea generation to take my story from simply being an epic adventure to being truly Legendary.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/151-hark","name":"Hark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar151_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"932"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-11T13:50:55-07:00","id":132762,"title":"","content":"If recall correctly they are a few just based on local history, first you have the near collapse of the local wildlife in the park due to poor animal management, in allowing to many of certain species to be hunted the park was at one point largely empty of large mammals. The Second is that the hotsprings are regarded as sacred and places where the Spirits are close by the local native american tribes, many of which were killed and forcible removed from the park (they are still not allowed to visit the area, from what I can remember), when the park was created. Hope that helps.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2099-brothersale","name":"brothersale","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2099_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-11T18:16:05-07:00","id":132958,"title":"","content":"That's definitely correct in terms of the dynamics of wolf and elk populations in Yellowstone. Elk are by far the dominant herbivore, with a population of roughly 15,000. The next most numerous herbivore, Bison, have a population of about 4,000. Elk also make up about 95% of the diet of the now-reintroduced wolves in yellowstone. A related problem you could incorporate is the control of the bacterial disease Brucellosis, which is dramatically impacting the animals of the park.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Games are a way for us to explore what it means to be human, or inhuman, as the case may be. Let's all have a good time playing them.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/400-guitarsniper","name":"g